course: everyday
lesson: 342
title: Breaking things and hiding the pieces
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Jamie in Glasgow broke Mum's favourite mug and hid the pieces in the bin. She found them when she emptied it, and she was sad about the mug but sadder that he hid it. He said sorry, saved up for a new one, and at confession felt the weight lift. Things can be replaced; hiding is what makes it worse.
explain: Weeks later he slipped, worse than before. Bored on a dark evening and egged on by older boys, he threw a firecracker at old Mrs Reid's door. Her heart raced, her dog hid under the bed for a whole day, and the porch boards were scorched. A police officer came round and gave him a warning, Dad paid for the repair, and for a while the street looked at Jamie differently.
explain: The shame was heavy. He went to confession again, and the priest said what Mum said: everyone slips, forgiveness does not run out, but each time you must put it right. So Jamie said sorry to Mrs Reid at her door, painted her porch on Saturdays and walked her dog after school for a month.
explain: He also learned how habits change: slowly, with fewer silly friends and more to do than to be idle. He joined the football club, and the older boys stopped calling.
explain: Hidden damage usually gets worse: juice on a laptop wiped and kept secret ruined the keyboard the next day, while telling straight away might have saved it.
fact: Hiding something you broke usually upsets people more than the breaking itself.
fact: Some accidents get worse the longer they are hidden, like a spill inside a laptop.
fact: Running away from damage you caused doesn't make it go away; owning up does the most good.
fact: An accident is easy to forgive; a lie about it is harder.
fact: When nobody owns up at school, often the whole class is kept in.
fact: A firecracker at someone's door can frighten people and pets badly and damage the house; the police can be called.
fact: Making amends can be work as well as money, like painting a porch you damaged.
fact: People who have confessed can slip again; forgiveness does not run out, but each wrong must be put right.
fact: Habits change slowly: fewer friends who lead you into trouble and more things to do than to be idle.
fact: Owning up early usually saves everyone a lot of trouble.
exercise: Why did Jamie hide the pieces of the mug? | He was afraid, because Mum loved that mug | He thought the mug was old rubbish nobody wanted | He wanted to surprise Mum | He didn't know it was broken
exercise: What hurt Mum more than the broken mug? | That Jamie hid it from her | That the bin was full again | That the mug cost a lot of money | That he used her mug at all
exercise: A boy kicks his ball through the neighbour's window and runs inside. The ball has his name on it. What is the lesson? | Running away doesn't make it go away; owning up does | Never write your name on a ball you play with near windows | Neighbours never find out | Windows are the neighbour's problem
exercise: A boy spills juice on the laptop, wipes it and says nothing. Next day the keyboard dies. What might telling at once have done? | Saved the laptop, since it could be dried quickly | Got him in less trouble, but the laptop was gone anyway | Nothing, laptops can't be saved | Made the juice taste better
exercise: A boy cracks his friend's tablet and says it was already cracked. Why is that worse than the accident? | An accident is forgivable; a lie about it is harder | Tablets are cheap to fix, so it hardly matters which it was | The friend would never check | Cracks are normal on tablets
exercise: A boy breaks a school window with a football and says nothing. The class is kept in at break. What was fair? | Telling the head teacher and saying sorry to the caretaker | Keeping quiet so only the class suffers a little, not him a lot | Waiting until someone else is blamed | Paying the caretaker secretly
exercise: Jamie threw a firecracker at Mrs Reid's door. Who was harmed? | Mrs Reid, her frightened dog and her porch | Nobody, it was only a loud bang and some smoke | Only the older boys | Only Jamie
exercise: Why did Jamie do it, weeks after his first confession? | He was bored and egged on by older boys | He thought it was part of a firework display | Mrs Reid had asked him to scare off the foxes | He wanted to help the dog get used to bangs
exercise: What happened after the firecracker? | A police warning, Dad paying for repairs, and the street looking at him differently | Mrs Reid thanked him, because she thought it was a firework show for her birthday | Nothing, as nobody saw | A prize for the loudest bang
exercise: How did Jamie make amends to Mrs Reid? | Said sorry, painted her porch and walked her dog | Told her the older boys did it, not him, and went home | Sent a card and kept away | Paid her one pound
exercise: What did the priest say when Jamie confessed a second, bigger wrong? | Everyone slips, forgiveness doesn't run out, but put it right each time | You can't be forgiven twice, so it is better to stay away from church | It doesn't matter now | Stay away from church
exercise: Which change helped Jamie most not to slip again? | Fewer silly friends and more to do, like football club | Promising never to go out again, ever, even to school clubs | Hiding at home every evening | Telling no one what happened
exercise: Dad paid for Mrs Reid's porch. Why was Jamie's work still needed? | Amends come from the one who did it, not only the money | Dad made him do it as revenge for the cost of the repairs | The money was not enough for paint | Mrs Reid wanted free help
exercise: Jamie got away with nothing, yet his first fall (the mug) was smaller. What does that show about slipping back? | A slip can be worse than the first wrong, so habits matter | Confession makes you never do wrong again after the first time | Every slip is smaller than the last | Second wrongs never matter
exercise: Two boys break the same thing. One tells at once; one hides it and is found out a week later. Who is likely trusted more afterwards? | The one who told at once | The one who hid it longer | Both of them the same amount | Neither of them, ever again
exercise: A girl pulls the head off her sister's doll by accident and hides it. Why does the sister cry more when she finds it? | The hiding hurt her more than the broken doll did | She wanted a new one anyway and was cross to wait | Dolls are expensive | She liked searching
test: Mrs Reid's dog won't come out from under the bed. «It was only a banger, mate» says an older boy. What's true? | It badly scared a lady and her dog and damaged her porch | It was harmless fun, a banger is only a noise and it's over in a second | Dogs like loud bangs | Only fireworks harm anyone
test: Neighbour, holding a ball: «This has JAMIE written on it. Know anything about my window?» What should Jamie do? | Own up, say sorry and help pay | Say someone stole his ball last week | Hide upstairs until the man goes away | Say the wind must have blown the ball
test: Mum, pale: «why is the keyboard sticky and dead??» What should the boy say? | «I spilled juice yesterday and didn't tell you. Sorry» | «It was like that already when I came home from school, honest» | «The cat walked on it» | «It's just old»
test: Older boys: «come on, we did it last week and no one got caught». What is the wise answer? | No, it hurts people, and I'm going to football | Fine, but at a different house this time | Fine, if no one got caught | Only if I don't throw it
test: Friend's mum on the phone: «My son says the crack wasn't there before he lent it.» What helps most now? | Say sorry, tell the truth and offer to pay | Stick to the story and say nothing more | Say her son did it | Hang up
test: Jamie: «I already confessed once, so I can't go again, it's too embarrassing». What did the priest teach? | Go again: forgiveness doesn't run out, and put it right too | He should wait a whole year before going back, so the priest forgets him | He's right, once is enough | Confession only works once
test: Head teacher: «The whole class stays in until someone owns up about the window.» What would a fair pupil who did it do? | Own up, so the others don't pay | Keep quiet, it's only one short break | Blame the caretaker for leaving it | Ask his friends to own up for him
test: Library lady: «This page is torn. Who had this book?» The girl did. What is best? | Tell her, so the book can be mended | Say it came torn from the shelf | Push the book back on the shelf | Tear the page out fully
test: Mrs Reid, cool: «So you're the boy.» What is the best way to begin putting it right? | Say sorry and offer to paint the porch and walk the dog | Say the older boys made him do it and he didn't want to | Run away again | Say it was only a joke
test: Which is TRUE about hiding damage? | It often makes the damage worse and the people sadder | It always keeps you out of trouble if you hide it well | It is fine if the thing was cheap | Things hidden are never found
test: Dad, about the scratched car: «Nobody saw anything? Really?» The scratch was from his son's bike. What is the brave choice? | «It was me with my bike. Sorry, can I help polish it?» | «Maybe a bird did it» | «Some kid on the street» | Say nothing and go out
test: Why did the street look at Jamie differently after the firecracker? | Trust in him was broken and has to be earned back | They thought he was brave and grown-up for doing it | They didn't know him | They liked loud bangs
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment of damage caused accidentally. | Concealment commonly aggravates both the damage and the distress of the owner | Concealment is permissible where the article is of low value and readily replaced | Concealment invariably prevents any consequence | Concealment has no bearing upon the trust of others
exam: Identify the principal harms resulting from the discharge of a firecracker at the door of an elderly neighbour. | Fear caused to the occupant and her animal, and damage to the property | No harm, the noise being of short duration and the door being undamaged | Harm solely to the person discharging it | Harm solely to the reputation of the street
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the teaching concerning a penitent who repeats a wrong after a former confession? | Forgiveness remains available, but reparation is required on each occasion | Forgiveness is available on a single occasion only | Repetition relieves the penitent of any duty of reparation | A second confession is prohibited
exam: Determine which measures are most conducive to the gradual alteration of a habit of misconduct. | The avoidance of companions who incite it and the provision of occupation for idle time | A single promise never to err again | The concealment of all future misconduct | Remaining entirely idle at home
